# Rust Coding Standards

## Error Handling
- Use `anyhow::Result` for application-level errors
- Use `?` operator for propagating errors
- Log errors with appropriate log levels: `error!`, `warn!`, `info!`, `debug!`, `trace!`
- Handle errors gracefully in async contexts

## Async/Await Patterns
- Use `tokio::select!` for handling multiple async operations
- Prefer `futures::try_join!` for concurrent operations that can fail
- Use `futures::future::join_all` for concurrent operations that don't need error handling
- Implement proper shutdown handling with signal handlers

## Module Organization
- Keep modules focused and single-purpose
- Use `pub mod` declarations in [src/lib.rs](mdc:src/lib.rs)
- Export commonly used types through [src/prelude.rs](mdc:src/prelude.rs)
- Use `use crate::prelude::*;` for standard imports

## Channel Communication
- Use `tokio::sync::broadcast` channels for one-to-many communication
- Implement proper channel health checking
- Handle channel send/recv errors gracefully
- Use typed channel data with enums

## Testing
- Place tests in [tests/](mdc:tests/) directory
- Use `#[cfg(test)]` for test-only code
- Mock external dependencies when possible
- Test both success and error paths
